{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_011CeuS4SQoYQWtNyVUStaUc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0136GuX75HnQKkdp6h8rFREv","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/bear/Documents/CoWork/bear-textbooks/books/anthropics/claude-code-101/03-hooks-skills-commands/cc-grading-skill-definition/scratch/feedback/mira.md","content":"# Feedback — Mira O.\n\n## 1. Mechanism — **pass**\nYou name the outlier ($400k) and show its effect on both statistics with real arithmetic: the mean jumps to $88.4k while the median stays at $54.5k. The reader sees exactly *why* the mean moves — one large value pulls the sum — and *why* the median does not — the middle position is unchanged by what happens at the tail. That is the mechanism, shown rather than asserted.\n\n## 2. Example — **pass**\nThe ten-graduate salary set is concrete, particular, and yours. The numbers are plausible, the outlier is motivated (one big offer, not a random spike), and the math is checkable. One small caveat: the \"from $54.5k to $54.5k\" framing is a little loose — with the outlier included the median of ten values is $54.5k, but the \"before\" comparison is really the nine-value median of $54k. The point survives (the median barely moves), but stating the comparison you're actually making would tighten the sentence.\n\n## 3. Argument — **pass**\n\"The mean gets dragged around by outliers; the median doesn't\" is a clean controlling sentence, and the paragraph defends it end to end. The closing line (\"So report the median when a single big value is doing all the pulling\") lands as a consequence of that thesis rather than a new claim, which is what you want.\n\n## 4. Precision — **pass**\nYou don't use \"resistant to outliers\" verbatim, but \"the mean gets dragged around by outliers; the median doesn't\" is a fair equivalent — the resistance idea is doing the work. You also avoid the \"average = mean\" slip. If you wanted to strengthen this criterion in a revision, naming the property (\"the median is *resistant* to outliers\") would give the reader the technical handle to carry into the next chapter.\n\n## Growth\nThe single revision that would most improve this piece is to state the resistance property by name — one added clause tying your mechanism to the term \"resistant to outliers\" turns a strong intuitive paragraph into one a statistics reader can quote.\n\n## Final grade\n**4/4 pass — meets every criterion.**\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":198,"cache_read_input_tokens":47656,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":198},"output_tokens":13,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"65af77fa-b16d-418d-9c40-2d9bc98bf000","uuid":"7cd0abcd-8bdc-486f-9876-a22afefdfe31","request_id":"req_011CeuS4RmcBaScMnrKMBQBh"}
